---
/** Company: what HLLMR Ventures is, how it operates, and a short founder note (the 2026-08 home-page bio, moved here). */
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHead from "@hllmr/brand/components/PageHead.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, FOUNDER, LINKS, LOCALITY, OWNER } from "../../lib/site";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About · HLLMR Ventures"
  description="HLLMR Ventures LLC is a Texas company where new ventures start: software, services, entertainment and more, each grown until it can stand on its own."
  sheet="About"
>
  <PageHead
    dwg="About"
    title="HLLMR Ventures"
    lede="A small Texas company that starts ventures in whatever field the opportunity is in, and grows each one until it can stand on its own."
  />

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap co">
      <dl class="spec ident">
        <dt>Entity</dt><dd>{OWNER}, a Texas limited liability company</dd>
        <dt>Based</dt><dd>{LOCALITY}</dd>
        <dt>Founder</dt><dd>{FOUNDER} · <a href={LINKS.founder}>dhellmer.com</a></dd>
        <dt>Contact</dt><dd><a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a></dd>
        <dt>Code</dt><dd><a href={LINKS.github} rel="noopener">github.com/HLLMR-VENTURES</a></dd>
      </dl>

      <div class="prose">
        <h2>How HLLMR Ventures works</h2>
        <p>
          HLLMR is the house name. Ventures start here under it, in any field: a software product, a service practice, a
          music and events business. Each one has to prove itself, and when it does it gets its own name, look and address.
          Some will stay in the house; some will leave for their own domain or company.
        </p>
        <h2>Principles, in every field</h2>
        <ol class="principles">
          <li><strong>See it work.</strong> A claim that something works is checked, not assumed, whether it is software, a sound system or a booking.</li>
          <li><strong>Keep the record.</strong> Decisions are written down with the options that were turned down.</li>
          <li><strong>A person decides.</strong> Tools and AI agents do a lot of the work. A named person is accountable for what ships.</li>
          <li><strong>Prove the way back.</strong> Every change has a tested way to undo it before it goes live.</li>
          <li><strong>Own what matters.</strong> The house runs its own infrastructure and keeps its own data.</li>
          <li><strong>Say the limits.</strong> Every case study ends with what it does not prove.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>From the founder</h2>
        <p>
          HLLMR Ventures is run by its founder, {FOUNDER}, who writes:
        </p>
        <p>I build control systems, and lately I build software the way I was trained to build control systems.</p>
        <p>
          Twenty years in building automation and integrated building systems: the controls, physical security, fire, and
          networks that run a building, and the commissioning discipline that proves they do what the drawings say. Before
          that, the Navy's nuclear program, where you assume nothing works until you have watched it work.
        </p>
        <p>
          Now I build software with AI coding agents every day, and the same discipline turns out to be the missing piece.
          An agent will report a task complete when it is not. A control nobody has watched deny anything is an assumption.
          A plan regenerated from the code launders drift into the record. None of that is new. It is the failure mode
          every commissioning engineer already knows, arriving in an industry that has not learned to distrust a status
          point yet.
        </p>
        <p class="sig anno">{FOUNDER}, founder · <a href={LINKS.founder}>dhellmer.com</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .co {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.8fr);
    gap: clamp(24px, 5vw, 72px);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .ident {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--text);
    background: var(--surface);
    padding: 4px 16px;
  }
  .ident > :nth-last-child(-n + 2) {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }
  .ident {
    border-top: 2px solid var(--text);
  }
  .prose h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }
  .principles {
    padding-left: 1.3em;
  }
  .principles li {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }
  .principles li::marker {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--accent);
  }
  .sig {
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
  }
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .co {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
